For the present, given how new NitroHack is, I would agree with this.
(By "new", I mean that it was only just recently released to the
public.  I understand you've been working on it for a while, but that
work did not culminate in a release -- and thus start exposing the
bugs that invariably get noticed when users get their hands on the
software -- until just a few weeks ago.)

Later, when the bug fixing settles down a bit, you may want to do a
more careful "stable" release occasionally, so that people who don't
want to live on the bleeding edge can get a more tried-and-true
version with fewer risks.  This would especially be a good thing to do
_prior_ to starting work on major new features and significant code
changes, as those tend to introduce the potential for new bugs as
well.

Currently, I think you can consider NetHack 3.4.3 to be the stable
release, and NitroHack 4.0.x is a development series introducing
significant new features and major code changes, so it's kind of
unstable at the moment, as would be expected.  I know I was not
surprised to find some bugs in the first couple of versions.  That's
normal.

When you feel like 4.0.x has become pretty solid and you're about
ready to start new feature work, you could do a stable release from
the 4.0.x line and then use 4.1.x numbers for the new feature work.
Or something like that.

Just my two cents.


